{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210728,"nonce":"gvTetQGKUM6r8UwJg4ITvPSgmwM=","title":"CallLocal recursive frame fix (fib)","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\nFixed the top blocker for tiered `fib` correctness.\n\n**Root cause**: `CallStaticSelf` was lowered to `CallLocal { target: L0 }` where L0\nwas placed AFTER the Prologue. Recursive `bl L0` skipped the prologue, so all\nrecursive calls shared the same stack frame. Spill slots at `[x29, -8]` etc. were\noverwritten by inner calls, corrupting the outer call's local variables.\n\n**Fix**: Added `fn_entry_label` placed BEFORE the Prologue in `lower()`. Changed\n`CallStaticSelf` to target `fn_entry_label` instead of `blocks[0]`'s label. Each\nrecursive call now re-executes the prologue, allocating a fresh stack frame.\n\n**Result**: fib(28) now correctly produces 317811 on every run. Performance: 0.029s\n(6x faster than standard Wren's 0.176s).\n\n**Why:** Recursive functions need fresh stack frames for each invocation — the\nprologue/epilogue handles frame allocation and x29/x30 preservation.\n\n**How to apply:** Any future direct-call lowering must ensure the call target\nincludes the function prologue, not just the entry block body.","files":null}]}